# PART 1 — SEMANTIC AUDIT

Audit only the seven PIT-admissible agents used in AMS-1.

Current set:

* Turtle
* Moving Average crossover
* Signal Rolling
* Policy Gradient
* Evolution Strategy
* Neuro-Evolution
* Novelty Search

Use the repository as source of truth.

Do not rely on names alone.

For every agent determine from code:

## A. Exact emitted output

For example:

* integer action;
* categorical action;
* score;
* position target;
* trade transition;
* portfolio allocation.

## B. Exact action semantics

Determine precisely what:

```text
BUY
HOLD
SELL
```

or equivalent actions mean.

Examples:

```text
BUY = open/increase long position
SELL = close existing long
SELL = establish short
SELL = reduce exposure
HOLD = retain current position
```

These meanings are not interchangeable.

## C. Position dependency

Determine whether action semantics depend on current portfolio state.

For example:

```text
SELL while long
```

may mean "exit long", not "predict negative return".

Likewise:

```text
BUY while short
```

may mean "cover short", not "predict positive return".

## D. Objective / reward

For learned agents, document exact optimization target.

Examples:

* next-period direction;
* cumulative portfolio return;
* risk-adjusted reward;
* trading P&L;
* terminal wealth;
* novelty objective;
* strategy-specific fitness.

Ask:

> Is this objective equivalent to predicting the sign of the next 5-session absolute stock return?

Usually it may not be.

## E. Information thesis

Classify the economic interpretation of each agent.

Possible examples:

```text
trend continuation
mean reversion
breakout
position management
reward-maximizing policy
risk reduction
volatility response
portfolio timing
```

Do not infer this from future returns.

Derive it from implementation.

